.TH SET_FILTER 3 "15 Mar 2023" "CB" "Library Functions"

.SH NAME
set_filter, set_filter_out \- Filter elements of a set based on a predicate

.SH SYNOPSIS
.IB #include <lib/set.h>

.IB int
.IB set_filter(const set_t* in, set_t* out, int (*pred)(const char*, size_t));

.IB int
.IB set_filter_out(const set_t* in, set_t* out, int (*pred)(const char*, size_t));

.SH DESCRIPTION
The \fBset_filter\fR and \fBset_filter_out\fR functions filter elements of a set based on a predicate. They take three arguments: an input set, an output set, and a predicate function. The predicate function takes a pointer to an element and its size as arguments and returns a non-zero value if the element should be included in the output set.

The \fBset_filter\fR function includes elements that satisfy the predicate, while the \fBset_filter_out\fR function includes elements that do not satisfy the predicate.

.SH ARGUMENTS
.TP
.IB in
The input set.
.TP
.IB out
The output set.
.TP
.IB pred
A pointer to the predicate function.

.SH RETURN VALUE
The function returns the number of elements added to the output set.

.SH EXAMPLES
The following example demonstrates how to use the \fBset_filter\fR and \fBset_filter_out\fR functions:

.nf
#include <stdio.h>
#include <lib/set.h>

int is_even(const char* x, size_t n) {
    return (x[0] - '0') % 2 == 0;
}

int main() {
    set_t set, filtered_set, filtered_out_set;
    char* elements[] = {"1", "2", "3", "4", "5"};
    int num_elements = sizeof(elements) / sizeof(elements[0]);

    set_init(&set);
    set_init(&filtered_set);
    set_init(&filtered_out_set);

    for (int i = 0; i < num_elements; i++) {
        set_add(&set, elements[i], strlen(elements[i]));
    }

    set_filter(&set, &filtered_set, is_even);

    printf("Filtered set (even numbers):\n");
    set_foreach(&filtered_set, it, x, n) {
        printf("%.*s\n", (int)n, x);
    }

    set_filter_out(&set, &filtered_out_set, is_even);

    printf("Filtered out set (odd numbers):\n");
    set_foreach(&filtered_out_set, it, x, n) {
        printf("%.*s\n", (int)n, x);
    }

    set_free(&set);
    set_free(&filtered_set);
    set_free(&filtered_out_set);
    return 0;
}
.nef